> "belonging to John", "the baker's" for "belonging to the baker", etc.
> 
> "It's" actually means "it is" or "it has".

I was taught that "its" lack of an apostrophe parallels the lack in 
"his" or "hers".  So, when typing "its/it's", I mentally substitute 
"his" or "hers" and see if the sentence still makes sense.


